<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alexander II of Epirus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 272–c. 255 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-ii-of-russia">Alexander II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> was king of Epirus from 272 BCE, the son and successor of Pyrrhus. He fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-ii-gonatas">Antigonus II Gonatas</a> for Macedonia and was briefly driven from his own kingdom before regaining it. He is usually identified with the Alexander named in the thirteenth rock edict of the Indian emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> among the Hellenistic kings reached by Ashoka's envoys of dhamma.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-ii-of-epirus.jpg" alt="Alexander II of Epirus" width="320" height="166" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: no artist credit · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Alexander_II_of_Epirus.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ashoka's thirteenth rock edict names the Hellenistic kings Antiochus II, Ptolemy II, Antigonus Gonatas, Magas of Cyrene and <b>Alexander of Epirus</b> as recipients of his dhamma missions, highlighting the empire's engagement with the wider world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.74,20.7&amp;year=-264" aria-label="Epirus on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5061 741 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Alexander II of Epirus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5511 1011h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5511 1011h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.74,20.7&amp;year=-264">What was here in 264 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
